y-digit again to ensure no step was missed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the thousands place. Let me recount:\n\nThousands place in first number is 8 (from 108,028,558: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 8 in the thousands place?), wait let's see:\n\nWait, the first number is 108,028,558. Breaking down:\n\nDigits: 1 0 8 , 0 2 8 , 5 5 8\n\nSo positions:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n8 (millions)\n\n0 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (ten thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n8 (ones)\n\nSecond number:290,673,047:\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n9 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n0 (hundreds)\n\n4 (tens)\n\n7 (ones)\n\nSo when adding thousands place: first number has 8 (thousands place), second has 3 (thousands place). 8+3=11. So that's correct, write 1, carryover 1 to the next (ten thousands place). \n\nTen thousands place: first number has 2, second has7. 2+7=9 plus the carryover 1 makes 10. So 0 with carryover 1. \n\nHundred thousands place: first has 0, second has6. 0+6=6 plus 1 is 7. \n\nMillions place: first has8, second has0. 8+0=8.\n\nTen millions: first has0, second has9. 0+9=9.\n\nHundred millions:1+2=3.\n\nSo putting all together: 3 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),8 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),6 (hundreds),0 (tens),5 (ones). So 398,701,605. That's correct. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 398,701,605.\n</think>\n\n",
