\n1 (ones)\n\nSo aligning each digit:\n\nLet me write both numbers with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 6 9 7 9 5 4 5 5 0\n\nSecond number: 0 1 0 4 1 6 1 4 5 1 (Wait, actually, the second number is 10 digits? Wait, 104,161,451 is 9 digits. Let me count:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones). So 9 digits. The first number is 9 digits as well. Wait, 697,954,550 is 9 digits. So when adding, they should align properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the place breakdown earlier. Let me write both numbers digit by digit from right to left (ones to higher places):\n\nFirst number: 697,954,550\n\nDigits (right to left):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0\n\n2 (tens):5\n\n3 (hundreds):5\n\n4 (thousands):4\n\n5 (ten thousands):5\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9\n\n7 (millions):7\n\n8 (ten millions):9\n\n9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSecond number: 104,161,451\n\nDigits (right to left):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1\n\n2 (tens):5\n\n3 (hundreds):4\n\n4 (thousands):1\n\n5 (ten thousands):6\n\n6 (hundred thousands):1\n\n7 (millions):4\n\n8 (ten millions):0\n\n9 (hundred millions):1\n\n10 (billions):0 (since it's only 9 digits, so leading zero)\n\nWait, actually, the second number is 104,161,451 which is 9 digits, so when aligned with the first number (also 9 digits), they should be:\n\nFirst number: 6 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nWait, so when adding, each digit position:\n\nStarting from the ones (position 1):\n\n0 +1 =1\n\nTens (position2):5 +5=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover 1\n\nHundreds (position3):5 +4=9 +1=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1\n\nThousands (position4):4 +1=5 +1=6 \u2192 write6, no carryover\n\nTen thousands (position5):5 +6=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands (position6):9 +1=10 +1=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1\n\nMillions (position7):7 +4=11 +1=12 \u2192 write2, carryover1\n\nTen millions (position8):9 +0=9 +1=10 \u2192 write0, carryover1\n\nHundred millions (position9):6 +1=7 +1=8 \u2192 write8, no carryover\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n1 (ones), 0 (tens), 0 (hundreds),6 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),1 (hundred thousands),2 (millions),0 (ten millions),8 (hundred millions)\n\nSo putting them together from left to right (billions to ones):\n\nThe highest place is hundred millions (since both numbers are 9 digits). The result's highest digit is 8 (hundred millions), followed by 0 (ten millions?), wait let me see:\n\nWait, the digits in order from highest to lowest (left to right):\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:0\n\nOnes:1\n\nWait, that would be 802,116,001?\n\nWait, let's structure it properly:\n\nHundred millions place:8\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:0\n\nOnes:1\n\nSo the number is 802,116,001. Let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding the numbers:\n\n697,954,550\n\n+104,161,451\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 697,954,550\n\nAdd 100,000,000 \u2192 797,954,550\n\nThen add 4,161,451:\n\n797,954,550 +4,161,451 = ?\n\n797,954,550 +4,000,000 = 801,954,550\n\nThen add 161,451:\n\n801,954,550 +161,451 = 802,116,001. \n\nYes, that matches. So the final answer should be 802,116,001. Wait, but let me check once more with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator-like addition:\n\n697,954,550\n\n+104,161,451\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0+1=1\n\n5+5=10 (carry 1)\n\n5+4 +1=10 (carry1)\n\n4+1=5 +1=6 (no carry)\n\n5+6=11 (carry1)\n\n9+1=10 +1=11 (carry1)\n\n7+4=11 +1=12 (carry1)\n\n9+0=9 +1=10 (carry1)\n\n6+1=7 +1=8\n\nSo the digits are 8 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So 802,116,001. That seems correct. I think that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 697954550 and 104161451?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: 0 + 1 = 1\n2. The tens place: 5 + 5 = 10 (write 0, carry over 1)\n3. The hundreds place: 5 + 4 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry over 1)\n4. The thousands place: 4 + 1 = 5 + 1 = 6 (no carry)\n5. The ten thousands place: 5 + 6 = 11 (write 1, carry over 1)\n6. The hundred thousands place: 9 + 1 + 1 = 11 (write 1, carry over 1)\n7. The millions place: 7 + 4 + 1 = 12 (write 2, carry over 1)\n8. The ten millions place: 9 + 0 + 1 = 10 (write 0, carry over 1)\n9. The hundred millions place: 6 + 1 + 1 = 8 (no carry)\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is 802116001.",
