 make any mistakes in the carryovers. Let me go through each digit again:\n\nStarting from the right:\nOnes: 4+1=5 \u2714\ufe0f\nTens:1+0=1 \u2714\ufe0f\nHundreds:5+5=10 (carry 1) \u2714\ufe0f\nThousands:1+4=5 +1=6 \u2714\ufe0f\nTen thousands:6+3=9 \u2714\ufe0f\nHundred thousands:0+3=3 \u2714\ufe0f\nMillions:9+7=16 (carry 1) \u2714\ufe0f\nTen millions:9+9=18 +1=19 (carry 1) \u2714\ufe0f\nHundred millions:9+3=12 +1=13 (carry 1) \u2714\ufe0f\nBillions:1 (from carry) \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo the digits are: 1 (billion), 3 (hundred million), 8 (ten million), 7 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 6 (thousand), 0 (hundred), 1 (ten), 5 (one). Wait a second, when I broke it down, the ten millions place after carryover was 19, so the digit there is 9, and carryover 1. Then the hundred millions place becomes 9+3+1=13, so 3 in that place and carryover 1. Then the billions place is 1. So the number is 1,387,396,015. Let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, 990,061,514 + 397,334,501. Let's split them into millions and the rest.\n\n990,061,514 is 990 million plus 61,514.\n\n397,334,501 is 397 million plus 334,501.\n\nAdding the millions: 990 + 397 = 1,387 million.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 61,514 + 334,501 = 396,015.\n\nSo total is 1,387,000,000 + 396,015 = 1,387,396,015. Yep, that matches. Okay, so the answer should be correct.\n</think>\n\n",
