      "text": " Okay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 324,293,279 and 667,035,324. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this step by step. First, I should probably write them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits by their place values. Let me visualize that.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number ends with a 9, and the second ends with a 4. Adding those together: 9 + 4 equals 13. So I write down 3 in the ones place and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 7 and the second has a 2. Adding those plus the carried-over 1: 7 + 2 + 1 = 10. So I put 0 in the tens place and carry over another 1.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 2 and the second has a 3. Adding them with the carry: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6. No carryover here, so the hundreds digit is 6.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 3 and the second has a 5. 3 + 5 = 8. That's straightforward, so 8 in the thousands place.\n\nNext up is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 9 and the second has a 3. 9 + 3 = 12. So I write 2 and carry over 1.\n\nMoving to the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 2 and the second has a 0. Adding the carried 1: 2 + 0 + 1 = 3. So 3 there.\n\nNow the millions place. The first number has a 4 and the second has a 7. 4 + 7 = 11. Write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nNext is the ten millions place. The first number has a 2 and the second has a 6. Adding the carry: 2 + 6 + 1 = 9. So 9 there.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place. The first number has a 3 and the second has a 6. 3 + 6 = 9. So that's the last digit.\n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me recount each place step by step carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 324,293,279\nSecond number: 667,035,324\n\nAligning them:\n\n        3 2 4 2 9 3 2 7 9\n      + 6 6 7 0 3 5 3 2 4\n      ---------------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n9 + 4 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nTens: 7 + 2 = 9, plus 1 is 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nHundreds: 2 + 3 = 5, plus 1 is 6 \u2192 6, no carryover\n\nThousands: 3 + 5 = 8 \u2192 8\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\nHundred thousands: 2 + 0 = 2, plus 1 is 3 \u2192 3\n\nMillions: 4 + 7 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nTen millions: 2 + 6 = 8, plus 1 is 9 \u2192 9\n\nHundred millions: 3 + 6 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nPutting all together: 9 9 1 3 2 8 6 0 3. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, let me write the digits in order from the last carryover to the first. Wait, maybe I messed up the order here. Let me list each digit position properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write each digit step by step again, carefully noting each place:\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 324,293,279 \u2192 breaking down each digit from right:\n\nPositions (starting from the right, position 1 is ones):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 9\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 7\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 3\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 9\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 4\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 2\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 3\n\nSecond number: 667,035,324 \u2192 same breakdown:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 0\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 7\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 6\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition 1: 9 + 4 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1\n\nPosition 2: 7 + 2 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nPosition 3: 2 + 3 = 5 + 1 (carry) = 6 \u2192 6, no carry\n\nPosition 4: 3 + 5 = 8 \u2192 8, no carry\n\nPosition 5: 9 + 3 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1\n\nPosition 6: 2 + 0 = 2 + 1 (carry) = 3 \u2192 3, no carry\n\nPosition 7: 4 + 7 = 11 \u2192 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition 8: 2 + 6 = 8 + 1 (carry) = 9 \u2192 9, no carry\n\nPosition 9: 3 + 6 = 9 \u2192 9\n\nSo compiling the digits from position 1 to 9 (right to left):\n\nPositions 1-9 digits: 3 (ones), 0 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo writing the number from highest to lowest place: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (ones). So the number is 991,328,603.\n\nWait, let me confirm that. Let's see:\n\nHundred millions place: 9\n\nTen millions: 9\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands: 2\n\nThousands: 8\n\nHundreds: 6\n\nTens: 0\n\nOnes: 3\n\nSo putting commas: 991,328,603. Hmm, that seems correct. Let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers as whole:\n\n324,293,279 + 667,035,324.\n\nLet me add 324,293,279 + 600,000,000 = 924,293,279\n\nThen add 67,035,324: 924,293,279 + 67,035,324.\n\nAdding 60,000,000 gives 984,293,279, then 7,035,324 more:\n\n984,293,279 + 7,000,000 = 991,293,279\n\nThen add 35,324: 991,293,279 + 35,324 = 991,328,603. Yes, that matches. So the answer is 991,328,603. Okay, so the reasoning steps are correct. I just need to format it into the template provided.\n</think>\n\n",
