 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits are 4 (ones), 5 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 2 (thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions), and the carried over 1 in the billions place. So the total should be 1,127,752,254?\n\nWait, let me check that again because adding such large numbers can be error-prone. Maybe I should do it another way to confirm. Let me add them using another method, like breaking them into parts.\n\nFirst number: 229,804,604\nSecond number: 897,947,650\n\nAdding the millions first: 229,000,000 + 897,000,000 = 1,126,000,000\n\nThen the remaining parts: 804,604 + 947,650. Let's add those:\n\n800,000 + 900,000 = 1,700,000\n\n4,604 + 47,650 = 52,254 (Wait, 4,604 + 47,650: 4,604 + 40,000 = 44,604; then +7,650 = 52,254. Yes.)\n\nSo total of the smaller parts: 1,700,000 + 52,254 = 1,752,254\n\nNow add that to the 1,126,000,000: 1,126,000,000 + 1,752,254 = 1,127,752,254. Okay, that matches my previous result. So the answer should be 1,127,752,254. Let me just confirm once more with another approach, maybe column addition again.\n\nStarting over:\n\n229,804,604\n+897,947,650\n-------------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4 + 0 = 4\nTens: 0 + 5 = 5\nHundreds: 6 + 6 = 12 (write 2, carry 1)\nThousands: 4 + 7 = 11 + 1 = 12 (write 2, carry 1)\nTen thousands: 0 + 4 = 4 + 1 = 5\nHundred thousands: 8 + 9 = 17 (write 7, carry 1)\nMillions: 9 + 7 = 16 +1 =17 (write 7, carry1)\nTen millions: 2 +9 =11 +1=12 (write 2, carry1)\nHundred millions: 2 +8 =10 +1=11 (write 1, carry1)\nBillions: carryover 1.\n\nSo writing all together: 1,127,752,254. Yep, same result. I think that's correct. So the final answer is 1,127,752,254.\n</think>\n\n",
