ds), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits.\n\nWait a second, maybe I should write it out step by step more carefully. Let me go through each digit position again, just to confirm.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8 + 0 = 8 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 3 + 1 = 4 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 3 + 6 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 8 + 1 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 0 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 7 = 12. So write down 2, carry over 1. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nMillions: 1 (from first number) + 1 (from second) + 1 (carry) = 3 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen millions: 7 (first) + 0 (second) = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred millions: 4 + 1 = 5 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSo combining all the digits from right to left: 5,7,3,2,9,9,9,4,8. Wait, but when written as a number, it should be 573,299,948?\n\nWait, let me check the positions again. Let's count the digits:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n471,598,338 has 9 digits: 4 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\n101,701,610 has 9 digits: 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8 + 0 = 8\n\nTens: 3 + 1 = 4\n\nHundreds: 3 + 6 = 9\n\nThousands: 8 + 1 = 9\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 0 = 9\n\nHundred thousands: 5 + 7 = 12 (carry 1, write 2)\n\nMillions: 1 + 1 + 1 (carry) = 3\n\nTen millions: 7 + 0 = 7\n\nHundred millions: 4 + 1 = 5\n\nSo the digits from right to left are 8,4,9,9,9,2,3,7,5. Wait, that would be 573,299,948? Let me write them in order:\n\nHundred millions: 5\n\nTen millions:7\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:8\n\nSo combining: 5 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (ones). So the number is 573,299,948.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers as whole:\n\n471,598,338\n\n+101,701,610\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 471,598,338 + 100,000,000 = 571,598,338\n\nThen add 1,701,610 to that:\n\n571,598,338 + 1,701,610\n\nBreakdown:\n\n571,598,338 + 1,000,000 = 572,598,338\n\nThen add 701,610:\n\n572,598,338 + 700,000 = 573,298,338\n\nThen add 1,610:\n\n573,298,338 + 1,610 = 573,299,948\n\nYes, that matches. So the final answer should be 573,299,948. Let me check once more with another approach, maybe column addition:\n\n          471,598,338\n\n        + 101,701,610\n\n        ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8+0=8\n\nTens:3+1=4\n\nHundreds:3+6=9\n\nThousands:8+1=9\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9\n\nHundred thousands:5+7=12 (write 2, carry 1)\n\nMillions:1+1 +1=3\n\nTen millions:7+0=7\n\nHundred millions:4+1=5\n\nSo yes, that gives 573,299,948. Okay, I think that's correct. I don't see any mistakes in the steps. The key was handling the carry-over correctly in the hundred thousands place. So the answer should be 573,299,948.\n</think>\n\n",
