ver) = 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 (first number) + 4 (second number) = 13. Since there's no carryover from the previous step, it's 13. So write 3 and carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 6 (first number) + 9 (second number) = 15 + 1 (carryover) = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nTen-millions place: 7 (first number) + 4 (second number) = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-millions place: 2 (first number) + 4 (second number) = 6 + 1 (carryover) = 7. Write 7.\n\nPutting it all together: 726,371,319? Wait, let me check again. Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me write each digit step by step.\n\nLet me list each digit position:\n\nFirst number: 2 7 6 9 0 3 4 9 5\n\nSecond number:4 4 9 4 6 7 8 2 4\n\nWait, actually, when written out fully:\n\nFirst number: 2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number:4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo aligning them:\n\nHundred millions: 2 +4 =6?\n\nWait, no, when adding, each digit must be added column by column from right to left. Let me write them vertically:\n\n          2 7 6 9 0 3 4 9 5\n        + 4 4 9 4 6 7 8 2 4\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes: 5 +4 =9\n\nTens:9 +2=11 (write 1, carryover 1)\n\nHundreds:4 +8=12 +1=13 (write 3, carryover1)\n\nThousands:3 +7=10 +1=11 (write1, carryover1)\n\nTen-thousands:0 +6=6 +1=7 (write7, no carryover)\n\nHundred-thousands:9 +4=13 (write3, carryover1)\n\nMillions:6 +9=15 +1=16 (write6, carryover1)\n\nTen-millions:7 +4=11 +1=12 (write2, carryover1)\n\nHundred-millions:2 +4=6 +1=7 (write7)\n\nSo the result is 7 2 6 3 7 1 3 1 9 ?\n\nWait, let me write the digits in order from left to right:\n\nHundred-millions place:7\n\nTen-millions:2\n\nMillions:6\n\nHundred-thousands:3\n\nTen-thousands:7\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:9\n\nWait, that would be 726,371,319? Let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, 276,903,495 + 449,467,824.\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n276,903,495 is 276,903,495\n\n449,467,824 is 449,467,824\n\nAdding the millions: 276,000,000 + 449,000,000 = 725,000,000\n\nThen the remaining parts: 903,495 + 467,824.\n\n903,495 + 467,824:\n\nAdding those:\n\n900,000 + 400,000 = 1,300,000\n\n3,495 + 67,824 = 71,319 (Wait, 3,495 + 67,824 is 71,319?)\n\nWait, 3,495 + 67,824:\n\n3,495 + 60,000 = 63,495\n\n63,495 +7,824 = 71,319. Yes.\n\nSo total of the smaller parts: 1,300,000 +71,319 = 1,371,319\n\nAdding to the millions total: 725,000,000 +1,371,319 = 726,371,319. Okay, so that matches the previous result. So the answer should be 726,371,319.\n\nWait, but let me confirm once more with another approach. Let's use calculator-like addition step by step.\n\n276,903,495\n\n+449,467,824\n\nStart adding each digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:5+4=9\n\nTens:9+2=11 (1, carryover1)\n\nHundreds:4+8=12 +1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\nThousands:3+7=10 +1=11 (1, carryover1)\n\nTen-thousands:0+6=6 +1=7 (7, no carryover)\n\nHundred-thousands:9+4=13 (3, carryover1)\n\nMillions:6+9=15 +1=16 (6, carryover1)\n\nTen-millions:7+4=11 +1=12 (2, carryover1)\n\nHundred-millions:2+4=6 +1=7 (7)\n\nSo the digits from right to left are 9,1,3,1,7,3,6,2,7. Wait, but when written left to right, it's 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 9 (ones). So 726,371,319. That seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 726,371,319.\n</think>\n\n",
